  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="slab-shuttering-content">
      <h2>Slab Steel & Shuttering Calculator – Normal & Advanced Estimation</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Slab Steel & Shuttering Calculator</strong> is an essential quantity surveying and site planning tool designed for civil engineers, contractors, carpenters, and building estimators. It accurately computes formwork centering area, bottom soffit contact plates, side edge shuttering boards, commercial 8×4 ft plywood sheet counts, Acrow staging props, and reinforcement steel requirements for concrete roof and floor slabs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our calculator supports both a <strong>Normal Mode (Contractor Site Takeoff)</strong> for rapid material procurement ordering and cost budgeting, and an <strong>Advanced Mode (BBS & Staging Detailing)</strong> for engineering quantity surveying complying with <strong>IS 456</strong>, <strong>IS 14687</strong>, and <strong>BS 5975</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Components of Slab Shuttering (Formwork Area)</h2>
      <p>
        A complete slab formwork estimation consists of three distinct contact areas:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Bottom Soffit Formwork:</strong> The horizontal contact area supporting the slab underside, equal to <code>Slab Length × Slab Width</code>, minus any duct or staircase cutout openings.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Outer Side Edge Shuttering:</strong> The vertical side forms running along the perimeter of the slab to retain wet concrete during casting:
          <br />
          <code>Side Shuttering Area = 2 × (Length + Width) × Slab Thickness</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cutout / Duct Opening Perimeter Formwork:</strong> Vertical edge stop boards needed around staircase openings or plumbing cutouts.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Commercial Formwork Materials Takeoff</h2>
      <p>
        On real construction sites, contractors do not simply order square footage—they order standardized modular materials:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Plywood Sheets (8 ft × 4 ft):</strong> Standard commercial film-faced shuttering plywood covers <strong>32 sq.ft (2.973 m²)</strong> per sheet. Account for <strong>5% to 10%</strong> cutting wastage for corner adjustments.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Steel Shuttering Plates (3 ft × 2 ft):</strong> Standard steel farma plates cover <strong>6 sq.ft (0.557 m²)</strong> each.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Timber Runners (Ballas / Joists):</strong> 4"×3" or 3"×2" wooden battens placed at <strong>0.5 m (20 inches)</strong> spacing to support plywood sheets without deflection under concrete wet weight.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shuttering Release Oil:</strong> 1 Liter of chemical de-moulding oil covers approximately <strong>28 m² (300 sq.ft)</strong> of formwork contact surface.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Staging Acrow Props & Base Sole Plates</h2>
      <p>
        Acrow staging props support the falsework grid. Standard spacing for residential floor slabs is <strong>1.0 m to 1.2 m (3.3 ft to 4.0 ft)</strong>:
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Rows along Length = ⌈Length ÷ Prop Spacing⌉ + 1</code>
        <br />
        <code>Rows along Width = ⌈Width ÷ Prop Spacing⌉ + 1</code>
        <br />
        <code>Total Acrow Props = (Rows along Length) × (Rows along Width)</code>
      </p>
      <p>
        Each vertical prop requires a wooden sole plate or steel base plate to distribute loads safely onto the supporting surface.
      </p>

      <h2>Reinforcement Steel Estimation: Normal vs Advanced</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Normal Mode (CPWD Volume Rate):</strong> Standard residential slabs consume <strong>80 kg to 90 kg of steel per m³ of concrete</strong> (approx. 1.0% volume). Commercial slabs consume <strong>100 kg/m³</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Advanced BBS Mode:</strong> Detailed Bar Bending Schedule accounting for short span main tension bars, long span secondary bars, clear cover deduction, and 2 × 9D end anchorage hooks.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- FAQ SECTION SLOT -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            1. How do I calculate slab shuttering area?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          The bottom soffit area is <code>Length × Width</code> (minus any opening cutouts), and the side shuttering area is <code>Perimeter × Slab Thickness</code>. Total shuttering is the sum of both contact areas.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. How many 8x4 plywood sheets do I need for a slab?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          One standard sheet of 8 ft × 4 ft plywood covers <strong>32 sq.ft (2.973 m²)</strong>. Divide your total shuttering area in square feet by 32 and add 5% for site trimming wastage.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. How many Acrow props are needed for slab staging?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Props are arranged in a grid at 1.0 m to 1.2 m (3.3 ft to 4.0 ft) intervals. The formula is: <code>(⌈Length ÷ Spacing⌉ + 1) × (⌈Width ÷ Spacing⌉ + 1)</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. What is the thumb rule for steel in an RCC slab?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Under standard CPWD guidelines, residential RCC slabs consume <strong>80 kg of steel per m³ of wet concrete</strong> (or 0.8 to 1.1 kg per sq.ft of floor area).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. How much shuttering oil is required?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          One liter of shuttering release agent typically covers <strong>25 to 30 m² (270 to 320 sq.ft)</strong> of formwork contact surface.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. How are cement bags calculated from concrete volume?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Wet concrete volume is multiplied by a dry shrinkage factor of <strong>1.54</strong> to get Dry Volume. For an M20 mix (1:1.5:3, total ratio = 5.5), cement volume is <code>Dry Volume × (1 ÷ 5.5)</code>. Multiplying by cement density (1440 kg/m³) and dividing by 50 gives the exact number of 50kg bags.
        </p>
      </details>

    </div>
